/*
 * utf_word.c — Unicode word-character classification.
 *
 * VENDORED, DO NOT EDIT. Extracted from libutf:
 *
 *     https://github.com/brazilofmux/utf
 *     commit 3d4cdaf
 *     src/classify.c and tables/word_tables.c  (Unicode 16.0)
 *
 * To refresh: rebuild libutf and re-extract. Nothing here is hand-maintained;
 * editing it would make the next refresh silently lose the change.
 *
 * Vendored rather than linked for the same reason as utf_width.c and
 * utf_nfc.c: `mdfix` must still build from committed sources with nothing but
 * a C compiler. Upstream keeps this pair compilable standalone for exactly
 * that, so nothing else comes with it.
 *
 * Two mechanical changes from upstream, re-applied on each refresh rather
 * than remembered:
 *
 *   1. Tables are `static` and live in this file, so they cannot collide with
 *      a build that also links libutf.
 *   2. The entry points are renamed `utf_*` -> `mdfix_*`, as
 *      `co_console_width` -> `mdfix_display_width` was in utf_width.c.
 *
 * This exists because mdfix asked for it (brazilofmux/utf#3). Two rules here
 * need to know whether a code point is a word character, and both had been
 * approximating it as "any byte >= 0x80" — which keeps `漢字_の_強調`
 * literal, correctly, but also reads `。@key` as an email address rather than
 * a citation. The set is Alphabetic + Nd + Mn + Mc, with Pc exposed
 * separately: a caller deciding *about* an underscore needs it not to already
 * count as part of the word.
 */

